00:00:19.322 --> 00:00:21.280
STEPHANIE WONG: Whether
you're a solo developer

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.430
or a multinational corporation,
there's a good chance

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:26.740
that you're looking for
a transparent, simple,

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:29.680
and predictable way to manage
your costs in the cloud.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.439
Luckily, Google Cloud Platform
gives you a simple way

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.230
to organize your resources
to make billing and cost

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:39.050
optimization a lot easier
for your cloud projects.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.340
Let's start with projects,
which are GCP's basic organizing

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.310
entity and form the
basis for using all GCP

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:48.100
services, including
managing APIs,

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.190
enabling billing, adding
and removing collaborators,

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.620
managing permissions,
and other metadata that

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.580
describes your workloads.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.680
You also have a
billing account, which

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.770
defines who pays for
projects and resources.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:02.940
Each project has a
single billing account

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.870
which defines roles and
permissions and, of course,

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:08.130
manages payments.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.980
Next, let's talk about labels.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.350
Those are ad hoc
key value pairs that

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:16.430
are assigned to resources,
like VMs and GCS buckets, which

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.470
are categorized in
billing, so that you

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:22.370
can assign specific cost centers
or teams to foot the bill.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.040
Now, for those of you who need
more fine-grained reporting

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.010
and details, you'll want to
look into enabling the BigQuery

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.390
billing exports.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.880
This will export your
invoices as tables

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.060
into BigQuery, our large-scale
analytics platform.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:35.840
Why?

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.970
You gain incredible
reporting capabilities

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:41.240
at a low cost with extremely
granular data about your usage

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.474
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:43.474 --> 00:01:45.140
While you do get an
invoice every month,

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.930
investigating particular
cost drivers and trends using

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.090
the BigQuery export
helps you understand

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.250
every nuance of every cost.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.290
It lets you use
familiar SQL to slice

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:57.622
and dice your billing data
for powerful analysis.

00:01:57.622 --> 00:01:59.080
If you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.430
check out the link
in the description

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:02.860
below, and don't forget to
subscribe to the Cloud YouTube

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:04.967
channel for more great
Next Rewind content.

00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:05.800
Thanks for watching.

